        "Consider a household that receives a transitory negative income shock and chooses how much to reduce consumption. In a standard buffer-stock model [5], the consumption response depends on the household's liquid assets and its access to credit and transfers. Before PMJDY, rural households without accounts held savings mainly in cash, jewellery and livestock, which are risky, illiquid or costly to convert, and relied on informal loans at high interest rates when savings ran out. Digital financial inclusion affects this problem through three channels.",
